module.exports = [
  { q: ["What is Express Entry?", "can you explain express entry", "how does express entry work in canada"], a: "Express Entry is Canada's online system for managing applications for three federal economic programs: the Federal Skilled Worker Program (FSWP), the Canadian Experience Class (CEC) and the Federal Skilled Trades Program (FST). Some provincial nominee streams are linked to it too. You create a profile, you're ranked with a points score, and IRCC regularly invites top candidates to apply for permanent residence. Getting into the pool isn't a guarantee — you need an Invitation to Apply (ITA) before you can submit a full PR application.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What are the steps in Express Entry?", "express entry process step by step", "whats the order of things for express entry"], a: "In broad strokes:\n1. Check that you qualify for at least one program (FSWP, CEC or FST).\n2. Take an approved language test and, if you studied abroad, get an Educational Credential Assessment (ECA).\n3. Create your online profile and enter the pool.\n4. Wait for a draw — if your score is high enough, you get an Invitation to Apply (ITA).\n5. Submit your full PR application with documents, biometrics, medicals and police certificates before the deadline.\nCheck canada.ca for current requirements and fees.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the CRS score?", "what does CRS mean", "how is my express entry score calculated"], a: "CRS stands for Comprehensive Ranking System. It's the points system used to rank candidates in the Express Entry pool. Points are based on factors like age, education, official language ability, work experience, and things like a provincial nomination or a sibling in Canada. Spousal factors can count too. The exact point values and rules get adjusted from time to time, so use the official CRS tool on canada.ca to estimate your score.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's the current CRS cut-off?", "what score do i need for an ITA", "latest express entry draw cutoff?"], a: "Cut-off scores change with every draw, so I won't quote a number that could be outdated. IRCC publishes the results of each round — the date, the program or category, the number of invitations and the lowest score invited — on canada.ca. Keep in mind that category-based draws (for example, for French speakers or certain occupations) often have very different cut-offs from general draws.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an ITA?", "i got an ITA what now", "what does invitation to apply mean"], a: "An Invitation to Apply (ITA) means IRCC has selected your Express Entry profile and invited you to submit a full application for permanent residence. Congratulations! You have a fixed deadline to submit, shown in your account, so start right away:\n- Gather police certificates and book your medical exam early.\n- Make sure everything matches what you declared in your profile.\n- Pay the fees and upload all required documents.\nIf your situation has changed, get advice before you submit.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Who qualifies for the Federal Skilled Worker Program?", "FSWP eligibility", "can i apply under federal skilled worker"], a: "FSWP is for skilled workers with foreign (or Canadian) work experience. In general you need:\n- At least one year of continuous, paid, skilled work experience in an eligible occupation\n- Approved language test results meeting the minimum level\n- Education, with an ECA if it's from outside Canada\n- Enough points on the FSWP selection grid\n- Proof of funds, unless you're exempt\nThe exact criteria can change, so confirm on canada.ca. For a personal assessment, a licensed consultant (RCIC) or lawyer can help.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the Canadian Experience Class?", "CEC requirements", "i worked in canada can i get pr through CEC"], a: "The Canadian Experience Class is for people who already have skilled work experience in Canada. Generally you need at least one year of skilled, paid work experience in Canada within the past three years, gained while you were legally authorized to work, plus language test results that meet the minimum. Self-employment and work done while you were a full-time student usually don't count. Check canada.ca for the current details, and talk to an RCIC or immigration lawyer if your work history is complicated.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the Federal Skilled Trades Program?", "FST program canada", "im an electrician is there a program for trades"], a: "The Federal Skilled Trades Program is for qualified tradespeople, such as electricians, welders, plumbers and cooks in certain categories. In general you need work experience in an eligible skilled trade, language test results that meet the minimum, and either a valid job offer in Canada or a certificate of qualification in your trade from a Canadian province or territory. It's managed through Express Entry. Requirements change from time to time, so confirm the current rules on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Does it cost money to create an Express Entry profile?", "is express entry profile free", "fee to enter the pool?"], a: "No, creating an Express Entry profile is free. You only pay government fees once you get an Invitation to Apply and submit your PR application. You will pay for things outside of IRCC along the way, like language tests, an Educational Credential Assessment, medical exams and police certificates. Be wary of anyone who says you must pay them to 'register' in Express Entry.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How long does an Express Entry profile last?", "does my profile expire", "express entry profile validity"], a: "An Express Entry profile is generally valid for up to 12 months. If you don't get an Invitation to Apply in that time, it expires and you can create a new one if you still qualify. Your language test and ECA also have their own validity periods, so make sure they're still valid when you're invited and when you apply.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What are category-based Express Entry draws?", "what is category based selection", "draws for french speakers or healthcare?"], a: "Besides general draws, IRCC can invite Express Entry candidates who meet a specific category, such as strong French-language ability or experience in certain in-demand occupations. You don't apply to a category separately — if your profile fits one, you can be picked in that category's draws. The list of categories and the eligible occupations can change, so check the current categories on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How can I improve my CRS score?", "tips to raise my crs", "my score is too low what can i do"], a: "Common ways people raise their score:\n- Retake your language test — language is often the biggest lever.\n- Add a second official language (French can help a lot).\n- Gain more skilled work experience.\n- Complete additional education and get it assessed.\n- Look into provincial nominee programs, since a nomination gives a large boost.\n- Check whether your spouse's factors would help if they're the principal applicant instead.\nNever inflate your profile — misrepresentation can lead to a 5-year ban.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I include my spouse and kids in my Express Entry application?", "does my family come with me on express entry", "dependants in express entry"], a: "Yes. You can include your spouse or common-law partner and dependent children in your PR application, and they get permanent residence with you. Generally a dependent child is under 22 and not a spouse or partner, or older but dependent because of a physical or mental condition. All family members usually need medical exams, and adults need police certificates — even if they're not coming with you.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Should I declare my family members who are not coming with me?", "do i need to list my kid who isnt immigrating", "non accompanying family member"], a: "Yes — you should declare all your family members, including a spouse or dependent children who won't come with you. They usually still need a medical exam. If you leave them out, you may not be able to sponsor them later as family members, and it could be treated as misrepresentation. Get advice from an RCIC or immigration lawyer if you have a complicated family situation.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need a job offer for Express Entry?", "can i apply express entry without job offer", "is job offer required"], a: "No, most Express Entry candidates don't have a job offer. A job offer is not required for FSWP or CEC. For the Federal Skilled Trades Program, you need either a valid job offer or a Canadian certificate of qualification in your trade. How job offers affect your score has changed over time, so check canada.ca for current rules. Be careful of anyone selling job offers — that's a common scam.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is proof of funds?", "how much money do i need for express entry", "settlement funds requirement"], a: "Proof of funds shows you have enough money to support yourself and your family after you arrive. It's generally required for the Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades programs, but not usually for the Canadian Experience Class or if you're authorized to work in Canada with a valid job offer. The amount depends on your family size and is updated regularly, so check the current figures on canada.ca. The funds must be available, transferable and not borrowed.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I use borrowed money as proof of funds?", "can a friend lend me money for proof of funds", "gift money for settlement funds"], a: "No — borrowed money doesn't count as proof of funds. The money must be yours (or held jointly with your spouse or partner), available when you apply and when you get your visa, and free of debts or obligations. Officers usually want official bank letters and statements showing your balance over time. A sudden large deposit may get questions, so be ready to explain where the money came from.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a Provincial Nominee Program?", "what is PNP", "how do provincial nominee programs work"], a: "Provincial Nominee Programs (PNPs) let provinces and territories nominate people who meet their local labour market needs. Each province runs its own streams, with its own criteria — some target workers with job offers, international graduates, entrepreneurs or specific occupations. A nomination isn't PR by itself: once nominated, you apply to IRCC for permanent residence. Quebec doesn't have a PNP because it runs its own selection system. Streams open and close often, so check each province's official site.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between base and enhanced PNP?", "base vs enhanced nomination", "enhanced pnp meaning"], a: "Enhanced PNP streams are linked to Express Entry. If you get an enhanced nomination, it's added to your Express Entry profile and gives you a large CRS boost that usually leads to an Invitation to Apply. You then apply for PR online through Express Entry.\n\nBase PNP streams are outside Express Entry. After the nomination, you send a separate PR application to IRCC, which usually takes longer. Either way, you still need to pass medical, security and criminality checks.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I apply to more than one province's PNP?", "can i apply ontario and alberta pnp same time", "multiple pnp applications"], a: "Generally you can express interest in more than one province's program, as long as you meet each one's criteria and you're honest in each application. Many provinces expect you to genuinely intend to live there. If you get a nomination from one province, you typically can't hold another at the same time. Rules vary by province, so read each program's terms carefully or ask an RCIC.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I have to live in the province that nominated me?", "can i move after getting pnp", "pnp intent to reside"], a: "When you're nominated, you're expected to genuinely intend to live in that province. Once you're a permanent resident, you have mobility rights under the Charter and can legally live anywhere in Canada. But if you never intended to live there and misrepresented that, it could cause problems. Be honest about your plans. If your plans change, talk to a licensed immigration consultant or lawyer before you apply.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the Atlantic Immigration Program?", "AIP canada", "immigration program for nova scotia new brunswick pei newfoundland"], a: "The Atlantic Immigration Program is an employer-driven pathway to permanent residence for skilled workers and international graduates who want to live in New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, Prince Edward Island or Newfoundland and Labrador. Generally you need a job offer from an employer designated by the province, plus endorsement from the province, before you apply for PR. Criteria change from time to time, so check canada.ca and the province's website.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How does immigration to Quebec work?", "is quebec immigration different", "do i use express entry for quebec"], a: "Quebec has its own selection system for economic immigrants. Express Entry and PNPs don't apply if you plan to live in Quebec. Instead, Quebec's immigration ministry (MIFI) selects you first and issues a Quebec Selection Certificate (CSQ). Then you apply to IRCC for permanent residence, which handles medical, security and criminality checks. Quebec's programs and intake periods change, so check Quebec's official immigration website.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a CSQ?", "certificat de selection du quebec meaning", "whats a quebec selection certificate"], a: "A CSQ (Certificat de sélection du Québec) is the document Quebec issues when it selects you as an immigrant. It confirms Quebec has chosen you, but it doesn't make you a permanent resident. With your CSQ, you then apply to IRCC for PR. The CSQ is for people who intend to live in Quebec, and it's usually valid for a limited time, so apply to IRCC promptly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is Arrima?", "arrima portal quebec", "how do i use arrima"], a: "Arrima is Quebec's online portal for skilled worker immigration. You submit a declaration of interest describing your profile, such as your education, work experience and French or English skills. Quebec then invites candidates who match its priorities to apply for selection. Being in Arrima doesn't guarantee an invitation. The programs and selection criteria evolve, so check Quebec's official immigration site for current details.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need to speak French to immigrate to Quebec?", "is french required for quebec", "can i move to quebec only speaking english"], a: "French is very important for Quebec immigration. Many of Quebec's economic programs require or strongly favour French ability, and it affects your chances of being selected. Once you live in Quebec, French is the language of most public services and many workplaces. Quebec offers free French courses for eligible immigrants through Francisation Québec. Check current language requirements on Quebec's official immigration site.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I move to Quebec if I immigrated through Express Entry?", "i got pr through express entry can i live in montreal", "moving to quebec after pr"], a: "As a permanent resident, you have the right to live anywhere in Canada. But when you apply, you're expected to genuinely intend to live outside Quebec if you used a federal or provincial program. If you always planned to live in Quebec, you should have gone through Quebec's selection. If your plans genuinely change after you land, that's different. If you're unsure, ask a licensed consultant (RCIC) or a lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a CAQ?", "do i need a CAQ to study in quebec", "certificat d'acceptation du quebec"], a: "A CAQ (Certificat d'acceptation du Québec) is Quebec's approval that you need before getting certain temporary permits for Quebec. If you'll study in Quebec, you generally need a CAQ for studies before applying for a federal study permit. Some temporary foreign workers in Quebec also need one. Check the Quebec immigration ministry's website for current rules.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a NOC code?", "how do i find my NOC", "what does NOC mean in immigration"], a: "NOC stands for National Occupational Classification. It's Canada's system for classifying jobs, and immigration programs use it to decide if your work experience is eligible. To find your code, search the NOC on Statistics Canada or canada.ca and compare the main duties to what you actually did — not just your job title. Pick the NOC whose lead statement and most of the main duties match your real work.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What does TEER mean?", "TEER categories explained", "is TEER 3 skilled"], a: "TEER stands for Training, Education, Experience and Responsibilities. It's how NOC groups jobs:\n- TEER 0: management jobs\n- TEER 1: usually a university degree\n- TEER 2: usually college or apprenticeship of 2+ years, or supervisory jobs\n- TEER 3: usually shorter college or apprenticeship, or several months of training\n- TEER 4 and 5: high school, short training or none\nFor Express Entry, TEER 0 to 3 are generally considered skilled.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an ECA?", "educational credential assessment", "do i need to get my degree assessed"], a: "An Educational Credential Assessment (ECA) checks that your foreign degree, diploma or certificate is valid and equal to a Canadian credential. You need it for the Federal Skilled Worker Program if you studied outside Canada, and it can also earn CRS points for other candidates. It must come from an organization designated by IRCC, such as WES. An ECA for immigration is generally valid for five years.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Which organization should I use for my ECA?", "wes or ices which is better", "who does eca for doctors"], a: "Use an organization designated by IRCC for immigration purposes — the list is on canada.ca. For most people, any of the general designated organizations will do; choose based on how easy it is to get documents from your school, timelines and cost. Some professions have specific requirements — for example, doctors and pharmacists must use the designated organization for their profession. An ECA is not the same as a professional licence.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Does an ECA mean I can work as an engineer in Canada?", "is eca same as licence", "my degree is assessed can i practice"], a: "No. An ECA is only for immigration. It shows your education is equivalent to a Canadian credential, but it doesn't license you to practise a regulated profession. Jobs like engineer, nurse, doctor, teacher or lawyer are regulated by provincial or territorial bodies, and you'll need to go through their licensing process. You can often start that process before you arrive.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Which language tests does IRCC accept?", "accepted english tests for canada immigration", "ielts or celpip?"], a: "For economic immigration, IRCC accepts specific tests:\n- English: IELTS General Training, CELPIP-General, and PTE Core\n- French: TEF Canada and TCF Canada\nMake sure you take the right version — for example, IELTS Academic isn't accepted for Express Entry. Results are generally valid for two years. The list of approved tests can change, so confirm on canada.ca before booking.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is CLB?", "canadian language benchmark meaning", "what is NCLC"], a: "CLB stands for Canadian Language Benchmarks, the national standard used to describe English ability on a scale from 1 to 12. The French equivalent is NCLC (Niveaux de compétence linguistique canadiens). Immigration programs set minimum CLB/NCLC levels for each skill — reading, writing, listening and speaking. Your test scores are converted to CLB levels using official conversion charts on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Is IELTS Academic accepted for Express Entry?", "can i use ielts academic", "i took academic ielts by mistake"], a: "No, IELTS Academic isn't accepted for Express Entry or most economic immigration programs. You need IELTS General Training (or another approved test like CELPIP-General, PTE Core, TEF Canada or TCF Canada). Note that study permits are different — schools may ask for IELTS Academic for admission. Double-check which test you need on canada.ca before booking.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How long are language test results valid?", "does my ielts expire", "celpip validity for immigration"], a: "For immigration, language test results are generally valid for two years from the test date. For Express Entry, they must be valid when you create your profile and when you submit your PR application. If your results expire before you apply, you'll need to retake the test. Check canada.ca if you're close to the deadline.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Does French help with immigration?", "should i learn french for pr", "do french speakers get extra points"], a: "Yes, French can help a lot. Strong French can earn additional CRS points in Express Entry, and IRCC has held draws for French-speaking candidates. There are also programs that encourage francophones to settle in French-speaking communities outside Quebec. For Quebec, French is central to selection. Check current programs and categories on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I lie about my work experience to get more points?", "how to make fake work experience letter", "can i buy a reference letter for express entry"], a: "I can't help with that — and it's not worth it. Fake documents or inflated experience count as misrepresentation, which can lead to refusal and a 5-year ban from applying, and possibly losing status later. Instead, document your real experience properly: get reference letters from employers listing your duties, hours, dates and salary. If a past employer can't provide one, ask an RCIC or immigration lawyer about acceptable alternative evidence.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's in an employment reference letter for immigration?", "what should my experience letter say", "reference letter format for express entry"], a: "A good reference letter is on company letterhead with the company's contact info and is signed by a supervisor or HR. It usually includes:\n- Your job title(s) and dates of employment\n- Hours worked per week\n- Salary and benefits\n- Your main duties in detail\nThe duties should honestly reflect what you did. Check canada.ca for exact requirements for your program.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How can I sponsor my spouse to Canada?", "spousal sponsorship how does it work", "i want to bring my wife to canada"], a: "If you're a Canadian citizen or permanent resident aged 18 or older, you can sponsor your spouse, common-law partner or conjugal partner for permanent residence. You agree to support them financially, and they must pass medical, security and criminality checks. You'll need to prove your relationship is genuine with evidence like photos, communication, joint finances and documents. Check canada.ca for current forms and fees, and consider an RCIC or immigration lawyer for complex cases.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between inland and outland spousal sponsorship?", "inland vs outland sponsorship", "should i apply inland or outland"], a: "Inland means your spouse or partner lives with you in Canada and usually stays in Canada while the application is processed. They may be able to get an open work permit while waiting.\n\nOutland means the application is processed through the outside-Canada stream — your partner can be abroad or in Canada. One key difference: outland applicants generally have appeal rights to the Immigration Appeal Division if refused; inland applicants don't. An RCIC or lawyer can help you choose.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What counts as common-law in Canada?", "common law partner definition immigration", "how long do we have to live together to be common law"], a: "For immigration, a common-law partner is someone you've lived with in a conjugal relationship for at least 12 continuous months. Short absences for work or family are usually fine. You'll need proof of living together, such as a shared lease, joint bills, the same address on IDs, and joint bank accounts. If you can't live together for reasons beyond your control, the conjugal partner category might apply instead.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a conjugal partner?", "conjugal partner sponsorship", "we cant live together or marry can i sponsor"], a: "A conjugal partner is someone you've been in a committed, marriage-like relationship with for at least a year, but you couldn't live together or marry because of significant barriers — for example, immigration restrictions, legal barriers to marriage, or sexual orientation being criminalized in their country. It's not for couples who simply chose not to marry or live together. This category needs strong evidence, so getting advice from an RCIC or lawyer is a good idea.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How long do I have to support my sponsored spouse?", "sponsorship undertaking length", "financial responsibility for spouse sponsorship"], a: "When you sponsor a spouse or partner, you sign an undertaking to support them financially. Outside Quebec, the undertaking for a spouse or partner generally lasts three years from the day they become a permanent resident. It continues even if you separate or divorce. If they receive social assistance during that time, you may have to repay it. Quebec has its own undertaking rules.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I sponsor my spouse if I was sponsored by a spouse?", "i got pr as a spouse can i sponsor new wife", "5 year rule sponsorship"], a: "Generally, if you became a permanent resident as a sponsored spouse or partner, you can't sponsor a new spouse or partner until five years after you became a PR. There are also other bars, like being in default on a previous sponsorship or receiving social assistance for reasons other than disability. Check canada.ca for full eligibility, and talk to an RCIC or immigration lawyer for your situation.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I prove my relationship is real?", "evidence for spousal sponsorship", "what documents show genuine marriage"], a: "Officers look at your whole relationship history. Helpful evidence includes:\n- Marriage certificate or proof of living together\n- Photos together over time, including with family and friends\n- Messages and call logs over the course of the relationship\n- Travel records of visits\n- Joint bank accounts, lease, bills or insurance\n- Children's birth certificates, if any\nQuality and consistency matter more than volume. Be honest — inconsistencies cause delays.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I get married to someone just for papers?", "marriage of convenience canada", "pay someone to marry me for pr"], a: "No — I can't help with that. A marriage or relationship entered mainly to get immigration status is a 'bad faith' relationship. It leads to refusal, a possible misrepresentation finding with a 5-year ban, possible loss of status later, and even criminal charges. If you're looking for a legitimate route, I'm happy to walk you through work, study or economic programs you might qualify for.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can my spouse work while the sponsorship is processing?", "open work permit for spousal sponsorship", "can my husband work while waiting for pr"], a: "In many cases, yes. Spouses and partners being sponsored from inside Canada can often apply for an open work permit, which lets them work for most employers while the PR application is processed. Eligibility depends on their status and the type of application. The rules have changed over time, so check current eligibility on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I sponsor my parents?", "how to bring my parents to canada", "parents and grandparents program"], a: "Canadian citizens and permanent residents can sponsor parents and grandparents for permanent residence through the Parents and Grandparents Program (PGP). Intake is limited: IRCC usually invites potential sponsors from an interest pool rather than accepting all applications. Sponsors must meet minimum income requirements for several years and sign a long undertaking (generally 20 years outside Quebec). Check canada.ca for current intake and income figures.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a super visa?", "super visa for parents", "can my mom stay long in canada"], a: "A super visa lets parents and grandparents of Canadian citizens or permanent residents visit for longer stays per entry than a regular visitor visa. It can be valid for up to 10 years with multiple entries. The applicant generally needs a letter of invitation, proof that the host meets a minimum income, valid private medical insurance that meets IRCC's requirements, and an immigration medical exam. Check canada.ca for current requirements and income figures.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Super visa vs PGP — what's the difference?", "should i apply super visa or sponsor parents", "difference between super visa and parent sponsorship"], a: "A super visa is temporary — your parents or grandparents visit for long stays but stay foreign nationals. They don't get health coverage, can't work, and need private insurance. Sponsorship through the PGP gives them permanent residence, with access to provincial health care and the right to live here, but intake is limited and you take on a long financial undertaking. Many families use a super visa while waiting for a PGP invitation.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I sponsor my brother or sister?", "sponsor sibling to canada", "can i bring my sister"], a: "In most cases, no — siblings aren't eligible for family sponsorship. The narrow exception is if you have no other living relatives you could sponsor and none in Canada (sometimes called the 'lonely Canadian' rule), or if your sibling is an orphan under 18 and not married. Having a sibling in Canada can, however, give a CRS bonus in Express Entry. Siblings may also qualify on their own through work, study or economic programs.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I sponsor my child?", "sponsor dependent child to canada", "bring my son to canada"], a: "Yes, Canadian citizens and permanent residents can sponsor their dependent children. A dependent child is generally under 22 and not a spouse or common-law partner, or 22 or older but dependent on you because of a physical or mental condition. If you're also sponsoring your spouse, children can be included in that application. Adopted children have specific processes. Check canada.ca and consider an RCIC or immigration lawyer for custody issues.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can a permanent resident sponsor family while living abroad?", "can i sponsor from outside canada as pr", "pr living outside canada sponsor spouse"], a: "Permanent residents generally must live in Canada to sponsor. Canadian citizens living abroad can sponsor a spouse, partner or dependent child, but must show they'll live in Canada once the person becomes a PR. These rules matter a lot, so check canada.ca or speak with an RCIC or immigration lawyer before you apply.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What happens if my spousal sponsorship is refused?", "sponsorship refused what now", "appeal spouse sponsorship refusal"], a: "Read the refusal letter carefully to understand the reasons. Options can include:\n- Appealing to the Immigration Appeal Division (usually available for sponsor-led applications, with strict deadlines)\n- Applying for judicial review at the Federal Court\n- Reapplying with stronger evidence that addresses the concerns\nDeadlines are short, so act quickly. An RCIC or immigration lawyer can tell you the best option.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I get a study permit for Canada?", "study permit steps", "i want to study in canada"], a: "In general:\n1. Get accepted by a Designated Learning Institution (DLI) — only DLIs can host international students.\n2. Gather documents: letter of acceptance, proof of funds, passport and any other documents currently required.\n3. Apply for a study permit online (usually before you travel).\n4. Give biometrics and do a medical exam if required.\nFor Quebec, you also need a CAQ first. Requirements change, so check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a DLI?", "designated learning institution meaning", "is my college a DLI"], a: "A Designated Learning Institution (DLI) is a school approved by a provincial or territorial government to host international students. All primary and secondary schools in Canada are DLIs, but post-secondary schools must be on the official DLI list on canada.ca. You need an acceptance letter from a DLI to get a study permit. Also check whether the program makes you eligible for a post-graduation work permit, since not all do.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I work while studying in Canada?", "how many hours can international students work", "off campus work rules for students"], a: "Many international students can work on campus and off campus without a separate work permit if their study permit allows it and they're enrolled full time in an eligible program. Off-campus work is limited to a set number of hours per week during academic sessions, with more flexibility during scheduled breaks. The weekly limit has changed in recent years, so check the current rule on canada.ca. You'll also need a SIN.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need a study permit for a short course?", "study in canada without permit", "6 month course do i need permit"], a: "Generally, you don't need a study permit if your course or program is six months or less and you'll finish it within the period of your authorized stay. You'd come as a visitor. But if you might continue into a longer program, it's often wise to get a study permit from the start. Check canada.ca for exceptions.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I bring my family on a study permit?", "can my spouse come with me while i study", "dependants for international students"], a: "Your spouse or partner and children may be able to come with you, but they need their own applications — for example, a visitor visa, a study permit for school-age children, or a work permit for your spouse. Spousal open work permit eligibility for students' spouses was narrowed recently and depends on your level and program of study. Check current rules on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I extend my study permit?", "study permit expiring what do i do", "renew study permit inside canada"], a: "Apply to extend your study permit online before your current one expires — ideally at least 30 days before. If you apply before it expires, you generally have maintained status and can keep studying under the same conditions while you wait. You may need a new letter of enrolment and proof of funds. If your permit has already expired, ask about restoring your status quickly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I change schools on a study permit?", "transfer DLI study permit", "switch college as international student"], a: "Generally, you must study at the DLI listed on your study permit. If you want to change schools, check current rules on canada.ca, since recent changes may mean you need a new study permit before changing to a different school. Keep your enrolment status up to date. Changing programs at the same school is usually simpler.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a PGWP?", "post graduation work permit", "can i work after graduating in canada"], a: "A post-graduation work permit (PGWP) is an open work permit for eligible international graduates of Canadian DLIs. It lets you work for almost any employer, and the Canadian work experience you gain can help you qualify for PR. The length usually depends on your program length and is up to three years. You can generally only get one PGWP in your lifetime. Eligibility rules have changed recently, so check canada.ca before choosing your program.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["When should I apply for my PGWP?", "pgwp deadline after graduation", "how long do i have to apply for pgwp"], a: "You generally must apply within 180 days of receiving confirmation that you've completed your program (like a final transcript or letter from your school). You must also have held valid student status at some point during that period. If your study permit expires before you apply, you may need to restore your status first. Check current requirements on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I work while waiting for my PGWP?", "working after graduation before pgwp approved", "pgwp pending can i work"], a: "If you apply for your PGWP before your study permit expires, and you met the requirements for off-campus work while studying, you can generally work full time while waiting for a decision. If your study permit has already expired, you usually can't work until the PGWP is approved. Check canada.ca for the current conditions.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I get a second PGWP?", "can i extend my pgwp", "pgwp expiring what now"], a: "Generally no — a PGWP is a one-time permit and usually can't be extended. When it's running out, people often look at:\n- Applying for PR (for example, CEC through Express Entry or a PNP)\n- An employer-specific work permit, often with an LMIA\n- A bridging open work permit, if they've already applied for PR\nIRCC sometimes announces temporary policies, so check canada.ca. An RCIC can help you plan.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's the difference between an open and employer-specific work permit?", "open work permit vs closed", "can i change jobs on my work permit"], a: "An open work permit lets you work for almost any employer in Canada, with a few exceptions. An employer-specific (closed) permit lists one employer, often a job and location. You can only work under those conditions, so changing jobs usually means a new permit first. Examples of open permits: PGWP, some spousal permits and working holiday permits.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an LMIA?", "labour market impact assessment", "does my employer need lmia"], a: "An LMIA (Labour Market Impact Assessment) is a document an employer gets from Employment and Social Development Canada (ESDC/Service Canada) to show that hiring a foreign worker won't hurt the Canadian labour market — basically, that no Canadian or permanent resident is available. With a positive LMIA, you can apply for an employer-specific work permit. Some jobs are LMIA-exempt, for example through trade agreements. The employer applies, not you.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Should I pay for an LMIA?", "employer asking me to pay for lmia", "buy lmia job offer"], a: "No. Employers aren't allowed to recover LMIA costs from workers, and selling LMIAs or job offers is a known scam and can be illegal. If someone asks you for money to get an LMIA or a job offer, it's a red flag. You can report fraud to ESDC or the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre. Look for real jobs through legitimate employers and job boards like Job Bank.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is International Experience Canada?", "working holiday visa canada", "IEC how does it work"], a: "International Experience Canada (IEC) lets young people from partner countries work in Canada temporarily. It has three categories: Working Holiday (open work permit), Young Professionals (employer-specific, career-related work) and International Co-op (internships for students). You create a profile, enter a pool, and may get an invitation to apply. Age limits, quotas and how long you can stay depend on your country, so check canada.ca for your country's details.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Am I eligible for a working holiday in Canada?", "which countries have iec", "can americans get a working holiday visa"], a: "It depends on your citizenship. IEC is only for citizens of countries that have an agreement with Canada, and age limits are usually between 18 and 30 or 35 depending on the country. Some people from non-partner countries can apply through a recognized organization. Use the eligibility tool on canada.ca to see if you qualify and what the current rules are for your country.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I extend my work permit from inside Canada?", "work permit extension", "my work permit is expiring"], a: "You can usually apply online to extend or change your work permit from inside Canada (form IMM 5710 is the inside-Canada work permit application). Apply before your current permit expires. If you do, you generally keep maintained status and can keep working under the same conditions while you wait for a decision, as long as you stay in Canada. You'll need supporting documents, like a new LMIA or job offer if required.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a bridging open work permit?", "BOWP", "my work permit expires but i applied for pr"], a: "A bridging open work permit (BOWP) lets some people keep working while they wait for a decision on their permanent residence application. It's generally for people who've applied for PR through certain economic programs and whose current work permit is about to expire. There are specific eligibility conditions, like the PR application having reached a certain stage. Check canada.ca for current criteria.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I work in Canada as a visitor?", "can i work on tourist visa", "work while visiting canada"], a: "Generally no. As a visitor, you can't work for a Canadian employer without a work permit. Some limited business activities don't count as work, like attending meetings or conferences for a foreign employer. If you want to work, you need the right work permit. Working without authorization can lead to removal and future problems. Check canada.ca for what's allowed.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I look for a job in Canada as a visitor?", "job hunting on visitor visa", "can i do interviews while visiting"], a: "Yes, you can generally look for work and attend interviews as a visitor. But you can't start working until you have a valid work permit or other authorization. Be upfront at the border about why you're visiting. Recent rules about applying for a work permit from inside Canada as a visitor can change, so check canada.ca for the current options.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can my employer hire me without an LMIA?", "lmia exempt work permits", "intra company transfer canada"], a: "Some work permits are LMIA-exempt. Common examples include intra-company transfers, some jobs covered by trade agreements like CUSMA, certain francophone mobility jobs outside Quebec, and open permits like PGWP or spousal permits. The employer often still needs to submit an offer of employment through IRCC's employer portal. The details are technical, so an RCIC or immigration lawyer can help pick the right category.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need a visa or an eTA to visit Canada?", "visa vs eta canada", "what's the difference between eTA and visitor visa"], a: "It depends on your passport. Citizens of visa-required countries need a visitor visa (temporary resident visa), which is placed in your passport. Citizens of visa-exempt countries need an eTA (electronic travel authorization) if they're flying to or transiting through a Canadian airport. US citizens generally need neither, just a valid passport. Use the 'find out if you need a visa' tool on canada.ca — and only apply through official government sites.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an eTA?", "electronic travel authorization canada", "do i need eta for driving into canada"], a: "An eTA is an electronic travel authorization for visa-exempt travellers flying to or through Canada. It's linked to your passport and is generally valid for up to five years or until your passport expires, whichever comes first. You don't need one if you're entering by land or sea. Apply only on the official Government of Canada site — third-party sites often charge extra fees.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I apply for a Canadian visitor visa?", "tourist visa canada application", "IMM 5257 form"], a: "Visitor visas are usually applied for online through your IRCC account. The main application form is the IMM 5257. You'll usually need:\n- A valid passport\n- Proof of funds for your trip\n- Your travel purpose, like an invitation letter or itinerary\n- Proof of ties to your home country, like a job, family or property\nYou'll usually need to give biometrics too. Check canada.ca for current fees and processing times.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How long can I stay in Canada as a visitor?", "how many months can a tourist stay", "visitor stay length canada"], a: "Most visitors can stay up to six months from the day they enter. But the border officer decides, and they may give you a shorter or longer period by stamping your passport or issuing a document. If there's no stamp or document, it's generally six months. Your visa's expiry date is not how long you can stay — it's only the last day you can enter.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My visa is valid for 10 years — can I stay that long?", "visa expiry vs stay length", "multiple entry visa how long can i stay each time"], a: "No. Your visa's validity is the window during which you can enter Canada, not how long you can stay. Each time you enter, your stay is usually up to six months unless the border officer sets a different date. A multiple-entry visa can be valid for up to 10 years or until your passport expires, whichever is first.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I extend my stay as a visitor?", "visitor record canada", "want to stay longer than 6 months"], a: "You can apply online for a visitor record to extend your stay. Apply before your current status expires — ideally at least 30 days ahead. If you apply on time, you generally have maintained status and can stay under the same conditions while you wait. You'll need to explain why you want to stay longer and show you can support yourself. A visitor record is not a visa, so if you leave, you may need a valid visa to return.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can a border officer deny me entry even with a visa?", "refused at the airport with visa", "visa doesn't guarantee entry"], a: "Yes. A visa or eTA lets you travel to Canada, but the final decision is made by a Canada Border Services Agency (CBSA) officer when you arrive. They check that you're admissible and that you'll leave at the end of your stay. Bring supporting documents like proof of funds, a return ticket and your invitation letter, and answer questions honestly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I write an invitation letter for a visitor?", "invitation letter for parents visit", "letter of invitation canada visa"], a: "A letter of invitation usually includes:\n- Your full name, address, phone and status in Canada (with proof)\n- Your relationship to the visitor\n- Their full name, date of birth and address\n- The purpose and length of the visit\n- Where they'll stay and who's paying\nIt helps, but doesn't guarantee approval — the visitor still needs to meet all requirements. Only include true information.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What are biometrics?", "do i need to give fingerprints for canada", "biometrics appointment"], a: "Biometrics means your fingerprints and a photo, which IRCC uses to confirm your identity. Most applicants for visitor visas, study and work permits, and permanent residence need to give them. Once you're asked, you book an appointment at a visa application centre or another approved location. Biometrics are generally valid for 10 years, so you may not need to redo them each time. Some age-based and other exemptions apply — check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need to give biometrics again?", "biometrics valid how long", "already gave fingerprints last year"], a: "Biometrics are generally valid for 10 years. If you've already given them for a past application and they're still valid, you usually don't need to give them again for a temporary residence application. Permanent residence applications may have different rules. Use the biometrics validity check on canada.ca to confirm.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an immigration medical exam?", "do i need a medical for canada", "upfront medical exam"], a: "Many applicants need an immigration medical exam, especially for permanent residence and for longer temporary stays or certain jobs (like health care or work with children). It must be done by an IRCC-approved panel physician — your regular doctor can't do it. The exam usually includes a physical, plus tests like a chest X-ray and blood work depending on your age. Results are usually valid for 12 months.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can my family doctor do my immigration medical?", "where do i do immigration medical", "panel physician near me"], a: "No — immigration medical exams must be done by a panel physician approved by IRCC. You can find one using the 'find a panel physician' tool on canada.ca. Bring your ID, glasses if you wear them, and any medical records for existing conditions. The physician sends the results directly to IRCC.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Will a health condition stop me from immigrating?", "medical inadmissibility", "my kid has a disability can we still immigrate"], a: "Most health conditions don't stop you from immigrating. You may be found medically inadmissible if a condition is a danger to public health or safety, or in some cases if it would cause 'excessive demand' on health or social services. Many family class and refugee applicants are exempt from the excessive demand rule. If you get a procedural fairness letter about a medical issue, an RCIC or immigration lawyer can help you respond.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need a police certificate?", "police clearance for canada pr", "which countries do i need police certificates for"], a: "For permanent residence, you generally need a police certificate from each country where you've lived for six months or longer since age 18 — that includes your home country. Each country has its own process and timeline, so start early. Some temporary applications may also ask for one. Check the country-specific instructions on canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["I have a criminal record — can I still come to Canada?", "DUI entering canada", "criminal inadmissibility"], a: "A criminal record, including impaired driving, can make you inadmissible to Canada. Options may include:\n- Deemed rehabilitation, if enough time has passed for certain offences\n- Applying for criminal rehabilitation\n- A temporary resident permit (TRP) for a specific reason to travel\nBe honest — hiding a conviction is misrepresentation. These cases are complex, so it's best to talk to an RCIC or immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I check my application status?", "where to see my ircc application status", "how to track my application"], a: "Sign in to your IRCC account or the online portal you applied through to see messages and status updates. For some application types, like citizenship, sponsorship and PR, there's also an online application status tracker. You can see estimated processing times on canada.ca. If you have a specific question, use IRCC's web form. Never share your login with anyone you don't trust.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How long will my application take?", "processing time for pr", "how long for visitor visa"], a: "Processing times vary by application type, country and how complete your file is, and they change often, so I won't quote a number. Check the processing times tool on canada.ca for the latest estimates. Submitting a complete application with clear documents is the best way to avoid delays.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My application is taking longer than normal — what can I do?", "application stuck beyond processing time", "ircc delay what to do"], a: "A few options:\n- Check your IRCC account for any requests you may have missed.\n- Make sure your contact info is up to date.\n- Send an inquiry through IRCC's web form.\n- Request your GCMS notes to see what's happening with your file.\n- Contact your Member of Parliament's office — they can sometimes make inquiries.\nIn some long delay cases, lawyers may go to Federal Court to request a decision.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What are GCMS notes?", "how do i get my gcms notes", "ATIP request immigration"], a: "GCMS (Global Case Management System) notes are IRCC's internal records about your file, including officer notes and decisions. You can request them through an Access to Information and Privacy (ATIP) request online. Generally the requester must be in Canada or a citizen/PR, so if you're outside Canada, someone in Canada can request them for you with your written consent. They can show why you were refused or what's pending.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a procedural fairness letter?", "i got a PFL", "ircc letter saying they have concerns"], a: "A procedural fairness letter (PFL) means an officer has concerns that could lead to refusal — like possible misrepresentation, inadmissibility or doubt about your documents — and is giving you a chance to respond before deciding. Take it seriously:\n- Read the concerns carefully.\n- Respond by the deadline with clear evidence.\n- Ask for an extension if you truly need it.\nGetting help from an RCIC or immigration lawyer is strongly recommended.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What happens if I misrepresent on my application?", "consequences of misrepresentation canada", "what if i lied on my application"], a: "Misrepresentation means giving false information or leaving out important facts, even by mistake. It can lead to refusal and a finding that you're inadmissible to Canada for 5 years, during which you can't apply for PR. If you have status, it could later be taken away. If you realize you made an error, it's usually better to correct it proactively. Get advice from an RCIC or immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["I made a mistake on my application — what should I do?", "how to fix error on ircc application", "typo in my application"], a: "Correct it as soon as possible. Depending on where your application is, you can often update information through your IRCC account or the IRCC web form. Explain the mistake and include correct documents. Being upfront is much better than having an officer find it, since an uncorrected error can look like misrepresentation. For serious errors, talk to an RCIC or lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is maintained status?", "implied status canada", "is maintained status the same as implied status"], a: "Maintained status (formerly called 'implied status') lets you stay in Canada after your permit expires, as long as you applied to extend it before it expired. You keep the same conditions while you wait for a decision — for example, if you could work, you can generally keep working under the same conditions. It ends if you leave Canada or your application is refused. 'Maintained status' is the official term now.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I travel while on maintained status?", "leave canada while waiting for extension", "if i leave canada do i lose maintained status"], a: "If you leave Canada while on maintained status, you lose it. You may be able to re-enter as a visitor if you're eligible, but you generally can't keep working or studying until your new permit is approved. If you need to travel, plan carefully and talk to an RCIC or immigration lawyer first.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My permit expired — what can I do?", "out of status canada", "restoration of status"], a: "If your status expired within the last 90 days, you may be able to apply to restore it. You must have met the conditions of your stay and you can't work or study while waiting, even if you could before. If it's been longer than 90 days, options are limited, and you may need to leave Canada. Act quickly and get advice from an RCIC or immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a PR card?", "permanent resident card", "do i need a pr card"], a: "A PR card is the official proof of your permanent resident status when you travel. You need it (or a PR travel document) to return to Canada on a commercial carrier like a plane, train, bus or boat. It's usually valid for five years. Important: your PR status doesn't expire when your card does — status is lost only through an official process or if you give it up.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I renew my PR card?", "pr card renewal", "my pr card is expiring"], a: "Apply to IRCC to renew your PR card, usually online, when it's close to expiring (check canada.ca for timing). To qualify, you must meet the residency obligation: at least 730 days physically in Canada within the last five years. A new card is usually valid for five years. If you've spent a lot of time abroad, get advice before applying, since an officer reviews your residency.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the residency obligation for PR?", "how many days do i need to stay in canada as pr", "730 days rule"], a: "To keep your permanent resident status, you must be physically present in Canada for at least 730 days (two years) within any five-year period. The days don't need to be continuous. Some time abroad can count, like accompanying a Canadian citizen spouse or partner, or working full time abroad for a Canadian business. Track your travel dates carefully.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I lose PR status if my card expires?", "expired pr card am i still pr", "pr card expired still permanent resident"], a: "No. An expired PR card doesn't mean you've lost PR status. You stay a permanent resident until there's an official decision taking it away or you voluntarily renounce it. But you'll need a valid card or a PR travel document to board a flight to Canada. Renew your card if you meet the residency obligation.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a PR travel document?", "PRTD", "im abroad and my pr card expired"], a: "A PR travel document (PRTD) is a document you apply for from outside Canada when you need to return and don't have a valid PR card. When you apply, an officer reviews whether you meet the residency obligation. If you don't, you may be refused, but you can explain humanitarian reasons and you may have appeal rights. Get advice from an RCIC or immigration lawyer if your residency is borderline.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I enter Canada by land without a PR card?", "drive into canada expired pr card", "border crossing without pr card"], a: "Permanent residents can generally enter at a land border without a valid PR card, because a PR has the right to enter Canada once status is confirmed. Bring other proof, like your old card or confirmation of PR. You may be asked about your residency obligation. Airlines and other commercial carriers, though, usually require a valid PR card or a PRTD.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What can't permanent residents do?", "pr vs citizen differences", "rights of permanent residents"], a: "Permanent residents can live, work and study anywhere in Canada, get most social benefits including health care, and are protected by the Charter. But PRs can't:\n- Vote or run for political office\n- Hold some jobs that need a high-level security clearance\nPRs also must meet the residency obligation and can lose status for serious criminality.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I become a Canadian citizen?", "citizenship requirements", "when can i apply for citizenship"], a: "In general, you need to:\n- Be a permanent resident\n- Have been physically present in Canada for at least 1,095 days in the five years before you apply\n- File taxes, if required, for enough years in that period\n- Pass a citizenship test and show language ability (if aged 18–54)\n- Attend a citizenship ceremony and take the oath\nUse the physical presence calculator on canada.ca before applying.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Does time before PR count for citizenship?", "time as student counts for citizenship", "half day credit citizenship"], a: "It can. Time you spent in Canada as a temporary resident or protected person in the five years before applying can generally count as half days, up to a maximum of 365 days toward the 1,095 days. Time as a PR counts as full days. Use the physical presence calculator on canada.ca — it does the math for you.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the citizenship test?", "citizenship test what's on it", "how to study for citizenship test"], a: "The citizenship test checks your knowledge of Canada — its history, geography, government, symbols, and the rights and responsibilities of citizens. It's for applicants aged 18 to 54. Study using IRCC's official study guide, which is free online. The test is multiple choice, and you usually take it online. If you don't pass, you'll typically get another chance.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do kids need to take the citizenship test?", "citizenship test age", "does my 60 year old mom need the test"], a: "No. Only applicants aged 18 to 54 need to take the citizenship test and prove language ability. Children under 18 and adults 55 or older don't need to take the test. Parents can include minor children in their application, or apply for them separately.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What language level do I need for citizenship?", "citizenship language requirement", "do i need ielts for citizenship"], a: "If you're 18 to 54, you need to show you can speak and listen in English or French at the level required (CLB/NCLC 4). Accepted proof includes approved language tests or certain education completed in English or French. Check the list of accepted proof on canada.ca before applying.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What happens at the citizenship ceremony?", "oath of citizenship", "what to expect at ceremony"], a: "The citizenship ceremony is the final step. You take the Oath of Citizenship, sign the oath form and receive your citizenship certificate. Ceremonies can be in person or virtual. You become a citizen only once you take the oath. Bring the documents listed in your invitation, usually your PR card and ID. After that, you can apply for a Canadian passport.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Does Canada allow dual citizenship?", "will i lose my original citizenship", "can i keep my passport if i become canadian"], a: "Canada allows dual or multiple citizenship, so you don't have to give up your other citizenship to become Canadian. But your other country may not allow it, and you might lose that citizenship automatically. Check with your home country's government or embassy before you apply.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I travel during my citizenship application?", "leave canada after applying citizenship", "travel before oath ceremony"], a: "Yes, you can usually travel while your citizenship application is in process, but plan carefully. You need to be available for the test, interviews and the ceremony, and to keep your PR card or PRTD valid to come back. Days abroad after you apply don't affect your physical presence count, but missing appointments can delay or end your application.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Is a baby born in Canada a citizen?", "birth in canada citizenship", "my child was born here"], a: "Yes. In general, anyone born in Canada is automatically a Canadian citizen, regardless of their parents' status. The main exception is children of foreign diplomats. You'll get a birth certificate from your province or territory and can then apply for a Canadian passport.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Is my child born abroad a Canadian citizen?", "citizenship by descent", "my kid was born outside canada"], a: "If at least one parent was a Canadian citizen when the child was born, the child may be a citizen by descent. The rules about how many generations can pass on citizenship outside Canada have changed recently, so check the current rules on canada.ca. If your child is a citizen, apply for a citizenship certificate (proof of citizenship). For complex family history, talk to an immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I claim refugee protection in Canada?", "how to claim asylum in canada", "where can i make a refugee claim"], a: "You can make a refugee claim at a port of entry (airport, land border or seaport) when you arrive, or from inside Canada online through IRCC. You'll be screened for eligibility, and eligible claims are referred to the Immigration and Refugee Board (IRB) for a hearing. Getting legal help early is important — many provinces offer legal aid for refugee claimants.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the IRB?", "immigration and refugee board", "who decides refugee claims"], a: "The Immigration and Refugee Board of Canada (IRB) is an independent tribunal. Its Refugee Protection Division hears refugee claims and decides whether someone is a Convention refugee or person in need of protection. Other divisions handle appeals and detention reviews, including the Immigration Appeal Division for some sponsorship refusals and residency appeals.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I claim asylum at the US-Canada land border?", "safe third country agreement", "crossing from US to claim refugee"], a: "Under the Safe Third Country Agreement, people arriving from the US at a land border are generally not eligible to make a refugee claim in Canada, and must claim in the first safe country they arrive in. There are exceptions, such as certain family members in Canada and unaccompanied minors. Since this applies along the whole land border, get legal advice before travelling.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a Basis of Claim form?", "BOC form refugee", "refugee claim documents"], a: "The Basis of Claim (BOC) form is where you explain why you're seeking refugee protection — who you fear, what happened to you, and why you can't get protection in your home country. It's a key document for your IRB hearing, and there are strict deadlines. Be complete and truthful, and get help from a lawyer or legal clinic if you can.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do refugee claimants get health care?", "health coverage for asylum seekers", "interim federal health program"], a: "Yes. Refugee claimants are usually covered by the Interim Federal Health Program (IFHP), which provides temporary health coverage until they're eligible for provincial health insurance or leave Canada. It covers basic health care and some extras. Bring your IFHP document to appointments and check if the clinic accepts it.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can refugee claimants work in Canada?", "work permit for asylum claimants", "can i work while my refugee claim is pending"], a: "Refugee claimants can often apply for a work permit while waiting for a decision, once eligibility is established and the medical exam is done. A study permit may also be possible. Check your IRCC account or canada.ca for the current process.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What happens if my refugee claim is refused?", "refugee claim rejected options", "appeal refugee decision"], a: "Depending on your case, options can include an appeal to the Refugee Appeal Division of the IRB, judicial review at the Federal Court, a pre-removal risk assessment, or a humanitarian and compassionate application. Deadlines are very short, so contact a lawyer or legal aid right away.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a humanitarian and compassionate application?", "H&C application canada", "apply for pr on humanitarian grounds"], a: "A humanitarian and compassionate (H&C) application lets people who don't fit a regular program ask for PR based on exceptional circumstances. Officers look at things like your ties to Canada, the best interests of any affected children, and the hardship you'd face if you had to leave. It's discretionary and complex, so talking to an immigration lawyer or RCIC is strongly recommended.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Who can legally charge for immigration advice?", "who is authorized to give immigration advice", "can anyone charge me to do my application"], a: "Only authorized representatives can charge a fee for immigration advice or representation:\n- Regulated Canadian immigration consultants (RCICs) licensed by the College of Immigration and Citizenship Consultants (CICC)\n- Lawyers and paralegals who are members of a Canadian provincial or territorial law society\n- Notaries who are members of the Chambre des notaires du Québec\nFamily or friends can help for free. Always verify licences on the regulator's official registry.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is an RCIC?", "regulated canadian immigration consultant meaning", "is my consultant licensed"], a: "An RCIC is a Regulated Canadian Immigration Consultant, licensed by the College of Immigration and Citizenship Consultants (CICC). RCICs can legally charge to advise you and represent you with IRCC. Before hiring one, look them up on the CICC's public registry to confirm their licence is active, and get a written service agreement.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a ghost consultant?", "unlicensed immigration agent", "my agent won't put his name on my application"], a: "A ghost consultant is someone who charges for immigration help but isn't authorized, and often hides their involvement by not listing themselves on your application. Red flags:\n- They won't sign forms as your representative\n- They promise guaranteed results or jobs\n- They ask you to lie or sign blank forms\nYou're responsible for everything in your application, so use only licensed representatives or apply yourself.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I need a consultant to apply?", "can i apply for pr myself", "is it necessary to hire a lawyer for immigration"], a: "No. You can apply on your own — IRCC's forms and guides are designed for applicants, and you don't get special treatment for using a representative. A licensed consultant (RCIC) or lawyer can help in complex situations, like past refusals, criminal records, medical issues or tricky family circumstances. If you hire someone, make sure they're authorized.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Is this call from IRCC real?", "someone says theyre from immigration and i'll be deported", "ircc phone scam"], a: "Probably not. IRCC won't call to threaten you with arrest or deportation, demand immediate payment, or ask for payment by gift cards, cryptocurrency, wire transfer or prepaid cards. Hang up, don't share personal info, and contact IRCC through official channels to check. Report scams to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre and your local police.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How can I spot immigration scams?", "fake job offer canada scam", "immigration fraud warning signs"], a: "Common warning signs:\n- Guaranteed visas, jobs or PR\n- Requests for payment by gift card, crypto or wire transfer\n- 'Special' access to IRCC or faster processing for a fee\n- Job offers that require you to pay for an LMIA or job\n- Unofficial websites or email addresses that look like government ones\nIRCC fees are paid through official government channels. When in doubt, check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["I paid a scammer — what do I do?", "got scammed by immigration agent", "how to report immigration fraud"], a: "Sorry this happened — act quickly:\n- Contact your bank or payment provider to try to stop or reverse the payment.\n- Report it to the Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre and your local police.\n- If an unlicensed person was involved, report them to IRCC or the CICC.\n- Check your IRCC account and change passwords if you shared logins.\nKeep all messages and receipts as evidence.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Are IRCC services free?", "is ircc asking for money legit", "how do i pay ircc fees"], a: "IRCC charges fees for most applications, but you pay them online through official government channels when you submit — not through someone who calls or messages you. Many forms and guides are free on canada.ca. Settlement services for newcomers are also free. If someone asks you to pay IRCC fees in an unusual way, it's likely a scam.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What are settlement services?", "free help for newcomers in canada", "newcomer services near me"], a: "Settlement services are free programs for newcomers, funded by the Government of Canada (IRCC). They can help you:\n- Find a job and get your credentials recognized\n- Take free English or French classes\n- Find housing and understand local services\n- Connect with your community\nMost permanent residents are eligible, and some services are available before you arrive. In Quebec, the provincial government funds and runs settlement services.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Are there free English classes for newcomers?", "LINC classes", "free french classes for immigrants"], a: "Yes. Eligible newcomers can take free language classes funded by IRCC, often called LINC (Language Instruction for Newcomers to Canada) in English or CLIC in French. Classes can be in person or online. In Quebec, Francisation Québec offers free French courses. Contact a local settlement agency to get assessed and registered.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I get settlement help before I arrive in Canada?", "pre arrival services", "help before landing in canada"], a: "Yes. IRCC funds free pre-arrival services for many people who've been approved for permanent residence. They can help with job search, credential recognition and planning your first weeks. Starting early can make your first months much smoother. Check canada.ca for the list of pre-arrival programs and who qualifies.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What should I do first after landing in Canada?", "first steps for new immigrants", "just landed what now"], a: "Welcome to Canada! Common first steps:\n1. Get your Social Insurance Number (SIN) from Service Canada.\n2. Apply for your provincial or territorial health card.\n3. Open a bank account.\n4. Get a Canadian phone number.\n5. Start building your credit history.\n6. Contact a free settlement agency.\n7. Look into exchanging your driver's licence.\nKeep your immigration documents in a safe place.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I get a SIN?", "social insurance number for newcomers", "where to apply for SIN"], a: "You apply for a Social Insurance Number (SIN) through Service Canada — online, in person or by mail. Bring your primary document, like your Confirmation of PR, PR card, or valid work or study permit. You need a SIN to work and to access some benefits. Keep it private and only give it when legally required, like to your employer or bank for tax reporting.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I get a health card?", "ohip for newcomers", "provincial health insurance how to apply"], a: "Health care is run by each province or territory, so you apply to your provincial plan, usually in person with your immigration documents and proof of address. Some provinces have a waiting period before coverage starts, so buy private health insurance to cover the gap. Check your province's health ministry website for the current rules.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I open a bank account as a newcomer?", "bank account new immigrant", "do i need credit history to open bank account"], a: "Yes. You can open a bank account with ID, such as your passport and immigration documents. You don't need a credit history. Many banks have newcomer packages with lower fees and help getting a first credit card. Compare offers and read the fine print before you sign up.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I build credit in Canada?", "no credit history new immigrant", "how to get a credit score in canada"], a: "Your credit history from another country usually doesn't transfer, so you'll start fresh. Ways to build credit:\n- Get a credit card (a secured or newcomer card is easier to get) and pay it in full each month.\n- Keep your balance low compared to your limit.\n- Pay bills like your phone on time.\nCheck your credit report for free from Canada's credit bureaus.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I use my foreign driver's licence in Canada?", "exchange drivers licence canada", "driving as a newcomer"], a: "Usually for a short period after you arrive, depending on your province. Some provinces let you exchange licences from certain countries without full testing, while others require road tests. The rules and time limits vary by province, so check your provincial licensing office soon after you arrive.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What documents should I bring when I land in Canada?", "landing documents pr", "what to carry at the port of entry"], a: "Keep these with you, not in checked bags:\n- Passport and visa (if required)\n- Confirmation of PR or permit approval letter\n- Proof of funds, if required\n- A list of goods you're bringing and goods to follow\n- Educational and work documents\n- Medical records and prescriptions\nAnswer the border officer's questions honestly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is Confirmation of Permanent Residence?", "COPR meaning", "what do i do with my copr"], a: "A Confirmation of Permanent Residence (COPR) is the document IRCC issues when your PR application is approved. You become a permanent resident when you complete landing — either at a port of entry or, if you're already in Canada, through IRCC's online process. Your COPR has an expiry date, so make sure to land before it expires. Your PR card is then mailed to you.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I update my address with IRCC?", "change address ircc", "i moved do i need to tell immigration"], a: "Yes, keep IRCC updated. You can usually change your address through your IRCC account or by using IRCC's web form. If you've applied for a PR card or citizenship, IRCC needs to be able to reach you. Missing a letter or interview can delay or close your application.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I withdraw my application?", "how to cancel ircc application", "withdraw pr application"], a: "Yes. You can ask IRCC to withdraw your application, usually through the web form or your account, as long as a final decision hasn't been made. Fees are usually only refunded if processing hasn't started. Think carefully before withdrawing, since you may lose your place in line.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My visa was refused — what can I do?", "visitor visa refusal reasons", "rejected trv what next"], a: "First, understand why. Read the refusal letter, and consider requesting your GCMS notes for detailed officer reasons. Then you can:\n- Reapply with stronger documents that address the concerns\n- In some cases, apply for judicial review at the Federal Court (strict deadlines)\nA refusal doesn't ban you from reapplying. Don't hide past refusals — you must declare them. For repeated refusals, consult an RCIC or immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I have to declare a previous refusal?", "past visa refusal other country", "should i mention refused us visa"], a: "Yes. If a form asks about past refusals — from Canada or any other country — answer truthfully. Not declaring a refusal can be treated as misrepresentation, which can lead to a 5-year ban. A past refusal doesn't automatically mean you'll be refused again. Explain the circumstances briefly and honestly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is a temporary resident permit?", "TRP canada", "inadmissible but need to enter canada"], a: "A temporary resident permit (TRP) lets someone who is otherwise inadmissible to Canada — for example, due to a criminal record — enter or stay temporarily for a specific purpose, when there's a strong reason. It's discretionary and doesn't fix the inadmissibility permanently. These cases are complex, so talk to an immigration lawyer or RCIC.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I visit the US with a Canadian PR card?", "pr card travel to usa", "does canadian pr let me go to the states"], a: "A Canadian PR card isn't a visa for the US. Whether you need a US visa depends on your citizenship, not your Canadian PR status. Check the US government's official rules before you travel, and remember time spent outside Canada counts against your residency obligation.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What's the Discover Canada guide?", "citizenship study guide", "where to study for citizenship exam"], a: "The citizenship test is based on IRCC's official study guide. It covers Canadian history, geography, government, symbols and the rights and responsibilities of citizenship. It's free online and in audio and other formats on canada.ca. Study it carefully — practice tests from unofficial sites can help but may be out of date.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do I have to file taxes to get citizenship?", "tax filing for citizenship", "citizenship and income tax requirement"], a: "Yes. If you were required to file income taxes under the Income Tax Act, you generally must have filed them for a certain number of years within the five years before you apply for citizenship. Make sure your tax filings are up to date before you apply. Check canada.ca for the exact rule.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can my PR be taken away?", "how do you lose permanent residence", "losing pr status"], a: "PR status doesn't expire automatically, but you can lose it if:\n- An officer finds you didn't meet the residency obligation and the decision is final\n- You're found inadmissible, for example for serious criminality or misrepresentation\n- You voluntarily renounce it\n- You become a Canadian citizen\nIf you're facing a residency or inadmissibility issue, get help from an immigration lawyer or RCIC quickly.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I renounce my PR status?", "give up permanent residence", "voluntary renunciation pr"], a: "Yes, you can voluntarily renounce your PR status by applying to IRCC. People sometimes do this if they've moved away permanently and want to visit Canada without residency questions. Once renounced, you become a foreign national and may need a visa or eTA to visit. Think it through, since getting PR again would mean a new application.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I sponsor my parents if I'm on a work permit?", "sponsor parents while not pr", "temporary resident sponsor family"], a: "No — only Canadian citizens and permanent residents can sponsor family members for permanent residence. On a work or study permit, your parents can apply as visitors (or for a super visa once you're a citizen or PR). Once you get PR, you can look into the Parents and Grandparents Program.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I apply for a study permit for my child?", "minor study permit canada", "kid going to school in canada"], a: "It depends on your status. Children of parents who are in Canada on certain study or work permits, or who are PRs or citizens, may be able to attend school without a study permit in some situations, though applying for one can still help. Minors coming to study alone usually need a custodian in Canada. Check canada.ca for current rules on minors.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I apply for PR while on a visitor visa?", "pr application while visiting canada", "visitor to pr"], a: "Being a visitor doesn't stop you from applying for PR if you qualify for a program, like Express Entry or family sponsorship. But visiting doesn't give you a path to PR on its own, and you can't work without authorization. Be honest about your intentions when you enter Canada. An RCIC or lawyer can help you check which route fits.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I keep working after my work permit expires if I applied to extend?", "work permit expired but extension pending can i work", "working on maintained status"], a: "Generally yes — if you applied to extend your work permit before it expired, you're on maintained status and can keep working under the same conditions (same employer, if it's employer-specific) until IRCC decides. Keep proof that you applied on time to show your employer. If you leave Canada or your application is refused, maintained status ends.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My study permit was refused — what now?", "study permit rejection reasons", "refused study visa can i reapply"], a: "Common refusal reasons include doubts about your finances, your study plan, or whether you'll leave Canada after studying. You can:\n- Request your GCMS notes to see the officer's reasoning\n- Reapply with stronger evidence that addresses each concern\n- In some cases, seek judicial review at the Federal Court (strict deadlines)\nDeclare the refusal on future applications. An RCIC or immigration lawyer can review your case.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can international students get PR?", "pr pathways after studying in canada", "student to permanent resident"], a: "Yes, many do, but a study permit doesn't lead to PR automatically. A common path is to graduate, get a PGWP, gain skilled Canadian work experience, and apply through the Canadian Experience Class or a provincial nominee stream for graduates. In Quebec, there are Quebec-specific pathways. Programs and requirements change often, so plan early and check canada.ca.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I immigrate to Quebec after studying there?", "quebec graduate pr pathway", "PEQ quebec"], a: "Quebec has had pathways for people who studied or worked in Quebec, such as the Quebec Experience Program (PEQ), along with its skilled worker programs. These programs, their requirements and their intake periods have changed often, and some have been paused at times. Check Quebec's immigration ministry (MIFI) website for what's open now, and consider an RCIC or a Quebec immigration lawyer.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["My medical exam expired before I landed — what now?", "medical exam validity expired copr", "do i need to redo medicals"], a: "Immigration medical results are usually valid for 12 months, and your COPR or visa expiry is often tied to them. If your medical expires before a decision, IRCC will usually tell you if you need a new exam. If your COPR has expired, contact IRCC through the web form right away — you generally can't land with an expired COPR.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What if I can't get a police certificate from a country?", "police certificate unavailable", "country won't issue police clearance"], a: "If you've genuinely tried and can't get a police certificate, keep proof of your attempts — like applications, receipts and emails. Include that proof and a written explanation with your application. The officer will decide whether to accept it or ask for something else. Check canada.ca for the country's specific instructions, since some have special procedures.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Do my documents need to be translated?", "translation for ircc documents", "certified translation canada immigration"], a: "Yes, documents that aren't in English or French usually need a translation. Generally you submit:\n- The translation\n- An affidavit from the translator, if they're not a certified translator\n- A certified copy of the original document\nYou, your family members, or your representative can't translate your own documents. Check your application guide on canada.ca for exact rules.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["What is the IRCC web form?", "how do i contact ircc", "ask ircc a question online"], a: "The IRCC web form is the official online way to ask questions about your application, send updates, or report changes like a new address or a mistake. It's on canada.ca. Give your application number, full name and date of birth so they can find your file. Never use third-party sites that claim to contact IRCC for you.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["I got married during my PR application — do I tell IRCC?", "new baby during pr processing", "family status change during application"], a: "Yes, you must tell IRCC about any change in your family — marriage, a new common-law partner, a birth, divorce or a death — before your PR is finalized. Newly added family members usually need medicals and background checks. Not reporting changes can be misrepresentation, and you might not be able to sponsor that person later. Use your account or the web form.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["How do I get a Canadian passport after citizenship?", "passport after citizenship ceremony", "can i travel on my pr card after becoming citizen"], a: "After your citizenship ceremony, apply for a Canadian passport using your citizenship certificate as proof. Once you're a citizen, your PR card is no longer valid for travel. Canadian citizens generally need a Canadian passport to fly back to Canada, so plan your travel around your passport timeline.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Can I apply for citizenship for my child?", "citizenship for minor child", "my child is pr can they become citizen"], a: "Yes. A parent can apply for citizenship for a child under 18 who's a permanent resident. If one parent is already a citizen or is applying at the same time, the child doesn't need to meet the physical presence requirement or take the test. Children under 14 don't take the oath. Check canada.ca for current details.", lang: "en", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Entrée express?", "comment ça marche entree express", "Peux-tu m'expliquer le système Entrée express?"], a: "Entrée express, c'est le système en ligne fédéral qui gère trois programmes économiques : le Programme des travailleurs qualifiés (fédéral), la catégorie de l'expérience canadienne et le Programme des travailleurs de métiers spécialisés. Certains volets des programmes provinciaux y sont aussi liés. Tu crées un profil, tu reçois un pointage, et IRCC invite régulièrement les meilleurs candidats à présenter une demande de résidence permanente. Entrée express ne s'applique pas si tu veux t'établir au Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le score SCG?", "crs ça veut dire quoi", "comment mon pointage entree express est calculé"], a: "Le SCG (Système de classement global, ou CRS en anglais) sert à classer les candidats dans le bassin d'Entrée express. Les points tiennent compte de facteurs comme l'âge, les études, les compétences en anglais et en français, et l'expérience de travail, plus d'autres éléments comme une désignation provinciale. Les valeurs exactes changent de temps en temps, alors utilise l'outil officiel sur canada.ca pour estimer ton score.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Quel est le score minimum du dernier tirage?", "c'est combien la note de passage entree express", "seuil SCG actuel?"], a: "Le seuil change à chaque ronde d'invitations, alors je préfère ne pas te donner un chiffre qui pourrait être dépassé. IRCC publie les résultats de chaque ronde sur canada.ca : la date, le type de tirage, le nombre d'invitations et le score le plus bas invité. Les tirages par catégorie (par exemple pour les francophones) ont souvent des seuils très différents des tirages généraux.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["J'ai reçu une invitation à présenter une demande, je fais quoi?", "c'est quoi une IPD", "invitation entree express prochaine étape"], a: "Félicitations! Une invitation à présenter une demande (IPD) veut dire que tu peux maintenant soumettre ta demande complète de résidence permanente. Tu as un délai fixe, indiqué dans ton compte, alors commence tout de suite :\n- Demande tes certificats de police et prends rendez-vous pour l'examen médical.\n- Vérifie que tout correspond à ce que tu as déclaré dans ton profil.\n- Paie les frais et téléverse tous les documents.\nSi ta situation a changé, demande conseil avant de soumettre.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que parler français aide pour immigrer?", "le francais donne-t-il des points", "tirages pour francophones"], a: "Oui, beaucoup! De bonnes compétences en français peuvent te donner des points supplémentaires dans Entrée express, et IRCC fait des tirages ciblant les candidats francophones. Il existe aussi des initiatives pour attirer les francophones dans les communautés francophones hors Québec. Au Québec, le français est central dans la sélection. Vérifie les catégories en vigueur sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Quels tests de langue sont acceptés?", "TEF ou TCF pour immigrer", "test de francais pour la residence permanente"], a: "Pour l'immigration économique, IRCC accepte :\n- En français : TEF Canada et TCF Canada\n- En anglais : IELTS General Training, CELPIP-General et PTE Core\nAssure-toi de passer la bonne version du test. Les résultats sont généralement valides deux ans. La liste peut changer, alors vérifie sur canada.ca avant de t'inscrire. Pour le Québec, consulte la liste des tests acceptés par le ministère québécois.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi les NCLC?", "niveaux de competence linguistique canadiens", "NCLC vs CLB"], a: "Les NCLC (Niveaux de compétence linguistique canadiens) sont la norme nationale pour décrire les compétences en français, sur une échelle de 1 à 12. L'équivalent anglais, ce sont les CLB. Les programmes d'immigration exigent un niveau minimal pour chaque habileté : compréhension orale, compréhension écrite, expression orale et expression écrite. Tes résultats de test sont convertis en NCLC selon des tableaux officiels sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi une EDE?", "evaluation des diplomes d'etudes", "dois-je faire evaluer mon diplome pour entree express"], a: "Une évaluation des diplômes d'études (EDE) confirme que ton diplôme étranger est valide et équivalent à un diplôme canadien. Elle est exigée pour le Programme des travailleurs qualifiés (fédéral) si tu as étudié à l'étranger, et elle peut te rapporter des points. Elle doit être faite par un organisme désigné par IRCC. Une EDE est généralement valide cinq ans. Attention : ce n'est pas un permis d'exercer une profession réglementée.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la CNP et les catégories FEER?", "code CNP comment le trouver", "FEER 3 c'est qualifié?"], a: "La CNP (Classification nationale des professions) classe les emplois au Canada. Les catégories FEER (formation, études, expérience et responsabilités) vont de 0 à 5 :\n- FEER 0 : gestion\n- FEER 1 : généralement un diplôme universitaire\n- FEER 2 et 3 : collège, apprentissage ou formation\n- FEER 4 et 5 : secondaire, courte formation ou aucune\nPour Entrée express, les FEER 0 à 3 sont généralement considérées comme qualifiées. Choisis ton code selon tes tâches réelles, pas juste ton titre.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la preuve de fonds?", "combien d'argent il faut pour immigrer", "fonds d'etablissement entree express"], a: "La preuve de fonds montre que tu as assez d'argent pour t'installer avec ta famille. Elle est généralement exigée pour le Programme des travailleurs qualifiés (fédéral) et celui des métiers spécialisés, mais habituellement pas pour la catégorie de l'expérience canadienne. Le montant dépend de la taille de ta famille et il est mis à jour régulièrement, alors vérifie les chiffres actuels sur canada.ca. L'argent ne peut pas être emprunté.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un programme des candidats des provinces?", "PCP c'est quoi", "programme provincial d'immigration"], a: "Les programmes des candidats des provinces (PCP) permettent aux provinces et territoires de désigner des personnes qui répondent à leurs besoins. Chaque province a ses propres volets et critères. Une désignation ne te donne pas la résidence permanente : tu dois ensuite faire ta demande à IRCC. Le volet « amélioré » est lié à Entrée express et donne un gros coup de pouce à ton score; le volet « de base » se fait hors Entrée express. Le Québec n'a pas de PCP : il a son propre système.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment immigrer au Québec?", "l'immigration au quebec c'est different?", "est-ce que je passe par entree express pour le quebec"], a: "Le Québec choisit lui-même ses immigrants économiques. Entrée express et les PCP ne s'appliquent pas si tu veux t'établir au Québec. Le processus en gros :\n1. Le ministère de l'Immigration, de la Francisation et de l'Intégration (MIFI) te sélectionne et te délivre un CSQ.\n2. Tu fais ensuite ta demande de résidence permanente à IRCC, qui vérifie la santé, la sécurité et la criminalité.\nLes programmes changent souvent, alors consulte le site officiel du gouvernement du Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un CSQ?", "certificat de selection du quebec", "a quoi sert le CSQ"], a: "Le CSQ (Certificat de sélection du Québec) est le document que le Québec te délivre quand il te sélectionne comme immigrant. Il confirme que le Québec t'a choisi, mais ce n'est pas la résidence permanente. Avec ton CSQ, tu présentes ensuite ta demande de résidence permanente au fédéral (IRCC). Le CSQ a une durée de validité limitée, alors ne tarde pas à faire ta demande.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Arrima?", "comment marche arrima", "portail arrima declaration d'interet"], a: "Arrima, c'est le portail en ligne du Québec pour l'immigration des travailleurs qualifiés. Tu y déposes une déclaration d'intérêt qui décrit ton profil : études, expérience, connaissance du français, etc. Le Québec invite ensuite les candidats qui correspondent à ses besoins à présenter une demande de sélection. Être dans Arrima ne garantit pas une invitation. Les programmes et critères évoluent, alors vérifie sur le site officiel du Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le PEQ?", "programme de l'experience quebecoise", "je suis diplomé au quebec je peux avoir la RP?"], a: "Le Programme de l'expérience québécoise (PEQ) a été une voie vers la sélection permanente pour certaines personnes qui ont étudié ou travaillé au Québec. Les conditions de ce programme et des autres programmes québécois ont souvent changé, et certains ont parfois été suspendus. Consulte le site du MIFI pour savoir ce qui est ouvert maintenant. Pour ton cas précis, un consultant réglementé en immigration canadienne (CRIC) ou un avocat en immigration peut t'aider.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un CAQ?", "j'ai besoin d'un CAQ pour etudier a montreal?", "certificat d'acceptation du quebec etudes"], a: "Le CAQ (Certificat d'acceptation du Québec) est l'autorisation du Québec dont tu as besoin avant certains permis temporaires. Si tu veux étudier au Québec, il te faut généralement un CAQ pour études avant de demander ton permis d'études au fédéral. Certains travailleurs temporaires en ont aussi besoin. Vérifie les règles actuelles sur le site du MIFI.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je dois parler français pour immigrer au Québec?", "francais obligatoire quebec immigration", "je parle juste anglais, je peux aller au quebec?"], a: "Le français est très important pour immigrer au Québec. Plusieurs programmes exigent ou favorisent fortement la connaissance du français, et ça influence tes chances d'être sélectionné. Au quotidien, c'est la langue de la plupart des services publics et de beaucoup de milieux de travail. Francisation Québec offre des cours de français gratuits aux personnes admissibles. Vérifie les exigences actuelles sur le site du Québec.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment parrainer mon conjoint?", "parrainage conjoint canada", "je veux faire venir ma blonde"], a: "Si tu es citoyen canadien ou résident permanent et que tu as au moins 18 ans, tu peux parrainer ton époux, ton conjoint de fait ou ton partenaire conjugal. Tu t'engages à subvenir à ses besoins, et la personne doit réussir les vérifications médicales, de sécurité et de criminalité. Tu dois prouver que la relation est authentique (photos, communications, finances communes, etc.). Au Québec, il y a aussi une étape provinciale. Pour un cas complexe, consulte un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un conjoint de fait pour l'immigration?", "union de fait definition", "combien de temps vivre ensemble pour etre conjoints de fait"], a: "Pour l'immigration fédérale, un conjoint de fait, c'est une personne avec qui tu vis dans une relation conjugale depuis au moins 12 mois consécutifs. De courtes absences pour le travail ou la famille sont généralement acceptées. Il te faut des preuves de vie commune : bail commun, factures, même adresse sur vos pièces d'identité, compte conjoint, etc.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Parrainage au Canada ou à l'étranger, quelle différence?", "parrainage interieur vs exterieur", "inland ou outland c'est quoi"], a: "Au Canada (inland) : ton conjoint vit avec toi au Canada et y reste pendant le traitement. Il peut souvent obtenir un permis de travail ouvert en attendant.\n\nÀ l'étranger (outland) : la demande passe par le volet hors Canada, même si ton conjoint est au Canada. Une différence importante : en cas de refus, le volet à l'étranger donne généralement un droit d'appel à la Section d'appel de l'immigration, pas le volet au Canada. Un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration peut t'aider à choisir.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux me marier juste pour les papiers?", "mariage blanc canada", "payer quelqu'un pour un mariage pour la RP"], a: "Non, je ne peux pas t'aider avec ça. Une relation conclue principalement pour obtenir un statut, c'est une relation de mauvaise foi. Ça mène au refus, à une possible interdiction de territoire de 5 ans pour fausses déclarations, à la perte du statut plus tard, et même à des accusations criminelles. Par contre, je peux volontiers t'expliquer les voies légales : travail, études ou programmes économiques.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux parrainer mes parents?", "programme des parents et grands-parents", "faire venir ma mere au canada"], a: "Oui, les citoyens et résidents permanents peuvent parrainer leurs parents et grands-parents dans le cadre du Programme des parents et des grands-parents (PPGP). Le nombre de demandes est limité : IRCC invite généralement des répondants à partir d'un bassin d'intérêt. Tu dois respecter un revenu minimal sur plusieurs années et signer un engagement de longue durée. Au Québec, il y a aussi des règles provinciales. Vérifie les chiffres actuels sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le super visa?", "super visa parents", "ma mere peut rester longtemps au canada?"], a: "Le super visa permet aux parents et grands-parents de citoyens canadiens ou de résidents permanents de faire des séjours plus longs que ceux d'un visa de visiteur ordinaire. Il peut être valide jusqu'à 10 ans, pour entrées multiples. Il faut généralement une lettre d'invitation, une preuve que l'hôte respecte un revenu minimal, une assurance médicale privée conforme et un examen médical. Vérifie les exigences et les montants actuels sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux parrainer mon frère ou ma sœur?", "parrainer ma soeur", "sponsor frere canada"], a: "Dans la plupart des cas, non : les frères et sœurs ne sont pas admissibles au parrainage familial. Il existe des exceptions étroites, par exemple si tu n'as aucun autre parent que tu pourrais parrainer et aucun membre de ta famille au Canada, ou s'il s'agit d'un orphelin de moins de 18 ans non marié. Par contre, ton frère ou ta sœur peut peut-être immigrer par un autre programme (travail, études, économique).", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment obtenir un permis d'études?", "etudier au canada etapes", "permis d'etudes canada comment faire"], a: "En général :\n1. Fais-toi accepter par un établissement d'enseignement désigné (EED).\n2. Rassemble tes documents : lettre d'acceptation, preuve de fonds, passeport et autres documents exigés.\n3. Présente ta demande de permis d'études en ligne.\n4. Donne tes données biométriques et passe l'examen médical si on te le demande.\nPour le Québec, il te faut d'abord un CAQ. Les exigences changent, alors vérifie sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux travailler pendant mes études?", "etudiant international travail hors campus", "combien d'heures un etudiant etranger peut travailler"], a: "Souvent, oui. Beaucoup d'étudiants internationaux peuvent travailler sur le campus et hors campus sans permis de travail distinct, si leur permis d'études le permet et qu'ils étudient à temps plein dans un programme admissible. Le travail hors campus est limité à un certain nombre d'heures par semaine pendant les sessions, avec plus de souplesse pendant les congés prévus. Cette limite a changé récemment, alors vérifie la règle actuelle sur canada.ca. Il te faut aussi un NAS.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un PTPD?", "permis de travail post-diplome", "je peux travailler apres mes etudes?"], a: "Le permis de travail postdiplôme (PTPD) est un permis de travail ouvert pour les diplômés admissibles d'un établissement canadien désigné. Il te permet de travailler pour presque n'importe quel employeur, et l'expérience acquise peut t'aider à obtenir la résidence permanente. Sa durée dépend généralement de la durée de ton programme, jusqu'à trois ans. Tu ne peux habituellement l'obtenir qu'une seule fois. Les critères ont changé récemment, alors vérifie sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Permis de travail ouvert ou fermé, c'est quoi la différence?", "permis ferme vs ouvert", "je peux changer d'employeur avec mon permis?"], a: "Un permis de travail ouvert te permet de travailler pour presque n'importe quel employeur, sauf quelques exceptions. Un permis lié à un employeur précis (« fermé ») indique un seul employeur, souvent un poste et un lieu. Pour changer d'emploi, il te faut généralement un nouveau permis d'abord. Exemples de permis ouverts : PTPD, certains permis pour conjoints et Vacances-travail.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi une EIMT?", "etude d'impact sur le marche du travail", "mon employeur a besoin d'une EIMT?"], a: "Une EIMT (étude d'impact sur le marché du travail) est un document que l'employeur obtient d'Emploi et Développement social Canada (EDSC/Service Canada). Elle montre que l'embauche d'un travailleur étranger n'aura pas d'effet négatif sur le marché du travail canadien. Avec une EIMT positive, tu peux demander un permis de travail lié à cet employeur. C'est l'employeur qui fait la demande. Au Québec, il y a aussi généralement une étape provinciale.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Mon employeur veut que je paie l'EIMT, c'est normal?", "acheter une EIMT", "payer pour une offre d'emploi au canada"], a: "Non. Les employeurs n'ont pas le droit de te faire payer les coûts de l'EIMT, et vendre des EIMT ou des offres d'emploi, c'est une arnaque connue, parfois illégale. Si quelqu'un te demande de l'argent pour une EIMT ou un emploi, méfie-toi. Tu peux signaler la fraude à EDSC ou au Centre antifraude du Canada. Cherche plutôt des emplois auprès d'employeurs légitimes, par exemple sur le Guichet-Emplois.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi Expérience internationale Canada?", "PVT canada", "visa vacances travail canada"], a: "Expérience internationale Canada (EIC) permet aux jeunes de pays partenaires de travailler temporairement au Canada. Il y a trois catégories : Vacances-travail (le fameux PVT, permis ouvert), Jeunes professionnels et Stage coop international. Tu crées un profil, tu entres dans un bassin et tu peux recevoir une invitation. Les limites d'âge, les quotas et la durée dépendent de ton pays, alors vérifie sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment prolonger mon permis de travail?", "mon permis de travail expire bientot", "renouveler permis de travail au canada"], a: "Tu peux habituellement présenter une demande en ligne de l'intérieur du Canada pour prolonger ou modifier ton permis de travail. Fais-la avant que ton permis expire. Si tu le fais à temps, tu as généralement le statut maintenu : tu peux continuer à travailler aux mêmes conditions en attendant la décision, tant que tu restes au Canada. Selon ton cas, il peut te falloir une nouvelle EIMT ou offre d'emploi, et au Québec, un nouveau CAQ.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi le statut maintenu?", "statut implicite c'est quoi", "mon permis a expire mais j'ai fait une demande"], a: "Le statut maintenu (qu'on appelait avant « statut implicite ») te permet de rester au Canada après l'expiration de ton permis, si tu as demandé une prolongation avant qu'il expire. Tu gardes les mêmes conditions en attendant la décision — par exemple, si tu pouvais travailler, tu peux généralement continuer. Le statut maintenu prend fin si tu quittes le Canada ou si ta demande est refusée.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Mon statut est expiré, qu'est-ce que je peux faire?", "rétablissement du statut", "je suis sans statut au canada"], a: "Si ton statut a expiré il y a moins de 90 jours, tu pourrais peut-être demander son rétablissement. Tu ne peux pas travailler ni étudier en attendant, même si tu le pouvais avant. Après 90 jours, les options sont limitées et tu devras peut-être quitter le Canada. Agis vite et consulte un consultant réglementé en immigration canadienne (CRIC) ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je peux travailler avec un visa de visiteur?", "travailler en tant que touriste au canada", "visiteur peut travailler?"], a: "En général, non. Comme visiteur, tu ne peux pas travailler pour un employeur canadien sans permis de travail. Certaines activités d'affaires limitées ne comptent pas comme du travail, comme assister à des réunions pour un employeur étranger. Travailler sans autorisation peut mener à ton renvoi et nuire à tes futures demandes. Vérifie ce qui est permis sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Visa ou AVE, j'ai besoin de quoi pour visiter le Canada?", "difference entre visa et AVE", "c'est quoi une AVE"], a: "Ça dépend de ton passeport. Les citoyens de pays dont les ressortissants ont besoin d'un visa doivent obtenir un visa de résident temporaire (visa de visiteur). Ceux des pays dispensés de visa ont besoin d'une AVE (autorisation de voyage électronique) s'ils arrivent ou transitent par avion. L'AVE est généralement valide jusqu'à cinq ans ou jusqu'à l'expiration du passeport. Utilise l'outil sur canada.ca et fais ta demande seulement sur le site officiel du gouvernement.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Combien de temps je peux rester comme visiteur?", "duree de sejour touriste canada", "6 mois visiteur?"], a: "La plupart des visiteurs peuvent rester jusqu'à six mois à partir de leur entrée. Mais c'est l'agent des services frontaliers qui décide : il peut t'accorder une période plus courte ou plus longue. Attention : la date d'expiration de ton visa, c'est la dernière date pour entrer au Canada, pas la durée de ton séjour. Pour rester plus longtemps, tu peux demander une fiche du visiteur avant la fin de ton statut.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi les données biométriques?", "empreintes digitales pour immigration canada", "rendez-vous biometrie"], a: "Les données biométriques, ce sont tes empreintes digitales et une photo, qu'IRCC utilise pour confirmer ton identité. La plupart des demandeurs de visa de visiteur, de permis d'études ou de travail et de résidence permanente doivent les fournir. Tu prends rendez-vous dans un centre de réception des demandes de visa ou un autre lieu approuvé. Elles sont généralement valides 10 ans. Il y a certaines exemptions, vérifie sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que je dois passer un examen médical?", "examen medical immigration", "medecin designe immigration canada"], a: "Beaucoup de demandeurs doivent passer un examen médical aux fins de l'immigration, surtout pour la résidence permanente et certains séjours temporaires plus longs ou certains emplois (santé, enfants). Il doit être fait par un médecin désigné par IRCC — ton médecin de famille ne peut pas le faire. Les résultats sont habituellement valides 12 mois. Tu peux trouver un médecin désigné avec l'outil sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que j'ai besoin d'un certificat de police?", "casier judiciaire pour la residence permanente", "certificat de police quels pays"], a: "Pour la résidence permanente, il te faut généralement un certificat de police de chaque pays où tu as vécu six mois ou plus depuis tes 18 ans, y compris ton pays d'origine. Chaque pays a sa propre procédure et ses délais, alors commence tôt. Certaines demandes temporaires peuvent aussi en exiger un. Consulte les instructions par pays sur canada.ca.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment vérifier l'état de ma demande?", "suivi de demande IRCC", "ou voir le statut de mon dossier"], a: "Connecte-toi à ton compte IRCC ou au portail que tu as utilisé pour voir les messages et les mises à jour. Pour certaines demandes (citoyenneté, parrainage, résidence permanente), il y a aussi un outil de suivi en ligne. Les délais de traitement estimés sont sur canada.ca. Pour une question précise, utilise le formulaire Web d'IRCC. Ne partage jamais tes identifiants.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Combien de temps prend ma demande?", "delai de traitement residence permanente", "c'est long combien un visa"], a: "Les délais varient selon le type de demande, le pays et la qualité de ton dossier, et ils changent souvent, alors je ne te donnerai pas de chiffre. Consulte l'outil des délais de traitement sur canada.ca pour les estimations les plus récentes. Envoyer une demande complète avec des documents clairs, c'est la meilleure façon d'éviter les retards.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi les notes SMGC?", "notes GCMS comment les obtenir", "demande AIPRP immigration"], a: "Les notes du SMGC (Système mondial de gestion des cas, ou GCMS) sont les notes internes d'IRCC sur ton dossier, y compris les commentaires des agents. Tu peux les demander en ligne par une demande d'accès à l'information et de protection des renseignements personnels (AIPRP). Si tu es à l'extérieur du Canada, une personne au Canada peut généralement faire la demande pour toi avec ton consentement écrit. Elles peuvent t'aider à comprendre un refus.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["J'ai reçu une lettre d'équité procédurale, c'est grave?", "lettre d'equite procedurale c'est quoi", "IRCC a des doutes sur mon dossier"], a: "Une lettre d'équité procédurale veut dire qu'un agent a des préoccupations qui pourraient mener à un refus (fausses déclarations possibles, interdiction de territoire, doute sur des documents) et te donne la chance d'y répondre avant de décider. Prends-la au sérieux :\n- Lis bien chaque préoccupation.\n- Réponds avant la date limite avec des preuves claires.\nIl est fortement recommandé de consulter un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Qu'est-ce qui arrive si je mens dans ma demande?", "fausse declaration immigration consequences", "j'ai oublie de declarer quelque chose"], a: "Une fausse déclaration, c'est donner de faux renseignements ou omettre des faits importants, même par erreur. Ça peut entraîner un refus et une interdiction de territoire de 5 ans, pendant laquelle tu ne peux pas demander la résidence permanente. Si tu as déjà un statut, il pourrait t'être retiré plus tard. Si tu as fait une erreur, mieux vaut la corriger toi-même rapidement. Parle à un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment faire une fausse lettre d'emploi?", "peux-tu m'aider a modifier mon releve bancaire", "acheter des faux documents pour immigrer"], a: "Je ne peux pas t'aider avec ça. Les faux documents, c'est de la fausse déclaration : refus, interdiction de territoire de 5 ans et parfois des accusations criminelles. Et c'est souvent détecté. Par contre, je peux t'aider à bien documenter ta vraie situation : lettres d'employeurs détaillées, relevés officiels, explications écrites. Si un document est impossible à obtenir, un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration peut te conseiller sur des preuves de remplacement.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi une carte RP?", "carte de resident permanent", "ma carte RP expire, je perds mon statut?"], a: "La carte de résident permanent (carte RP) est la preuve officielle de ton statut quand tu voyages. Tu en as besoin (ou d'un titre de voyage pour résident permanent) pour revenir au Canada par avion, train, autobus ou bateau. Elle est habituellement valide cinq ans. Important : ton statut n'expire pas avec ta carte. Tu restes résident permanent jusqu'à une décision officielle ou une renonciation volontaire.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Quelle est l'obligation de résidence pour un résident permanent?", "combien de jours au canada pour garder la RP", "regle des 730 jours"], a: "Pour garder ton statut de résident permanent, tu dois être présent au Canada au moins 730 jours (deux ans) sur toute période de cinq ans. Les jours n'ont pas besoin d'être consécutifs. Certains séjours à l'étranger peuvent compter, par exemple si tu accompagnes ton époux ou conjoint citoyen canadien. Note bien tes dates de voyage.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment renouveler ma carte RP?", "renouvellement carte resident permanent", "ma carte rp expire bientot"], a: "Tu fais une demande à IRCC, en ligne, quand ta carte approche de son expiration — vérifie sur canada.ca à quel moment présenter la demande. Tu devras montrer que tu respectes l'obligation de résidence (730 jours au Canada dans les cinq dernières années). Si tu as passé beaucoup de temps à l'étranger, demande conseil avant, car un agent va examiner ta résidence.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un titre de voyage pour résident permanent?", "TVRP", "je suis a l'etranger et ma carte rp est expiree"], a: "Le titre de voyage pour résident permanent (TVRP) est un document que tu demandes de l'extérieur du Canada si tu dois revenir et que tu n'as pas de carte RP valide. L'agent vérifie alors si tu respectes l'obligation de résidence. Si ce n'est pas le cas, ta demande peut être refusée, mais tu peux invoquer des motifs d'ordre humanitaire et tu pourrais avoir un droit d'appel. Si ta situation est limite, consulte un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment devenir citoyen canadien?", "conditions pour la citoyennete", "quand je peux demander la citoyennete"], a: "En général, tu dois :\n- Être résident permanent\n- Avoir été présent au Canada au moins 1 095 jours au cours des cinq années avant ta demande\n- Avoir produit tes déclarations de revenus, si tu devais le faire\n- Réussir l'examen et démontrer tes compétences en français ou en anglais (si tu as de 18 à 54 ans)\n- Prêter serment lors d'une cérémonie\nUtilise le calculateur de présence physique sur canada.ca avant de présenter ta demande.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que mon temps comme étudiant compte pour la citoyenneté?", "temps avant la RP citoyennete", "demi-journees citoyennete"], a: "Oui, en partie. Le temps passé au Canada comme résident temporaire ou personne protégée dans les cinq ans avant ta demande peut généralement compter pour une demi-journée par jour, jusqu'à un maximum de 365 jours sur les 1 095 jours requis. Le temps comme résident permanent compte en jours complets. Le calculateur de présence physique sur canada.ca fait le calcul pour toi.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi l'examen de citoyenneté?", "examen citoyennete canada", "comment se preparer a l'examen de citoyennete"], a: "L'examen de citoyenneté vérifie tes connaissances sur le Canada : histoire, géographie, gouvernement, symboles, droits et responsabilités des citoyens. Il s'adresse aux demandeurs de 18 à 54 ans. Étudie le guide officiel d'IRCC, gratuit en ligne. L'examen est à choix multiples et se fait généralement en ligne. Si tu échoues, tu as habituellement une autre chance.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment se passe la cérémonie de citoyenneté?", "serment de citoyennete", "ceremonie citoyennete en ligne?"], a: "La cérémonie de citoyenneté est la dernière étape. Tu prêtes le serment de citoyenneté, tu signes le formulaire et tu reçois ton certificat de citoyenneté. La cérémonie peut être en personne ou virtuelle. Tu deviens citoyen seulement une fois le serment prêté. Apporte les documents indiqués dans ton invitation. Ensuite, tu peux demander ton passeport canadien.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que le Canada accepte la double citoyenneté?", "je vais perdre ma nationalite si je deviens canadien?", "double nationalite canada"], a: "Oui, le Canada permet la double (ou multiple) citoyenneté. Tu n'as pas à renoncer à ta citoyenneté d'origine pour devenir Canadien. Par contre, ton autre pays ne le permet peut-être pas, et tu pourrais perdre cette nationalité automatiquement. Vérifie auprès du gouvernement ou de l'ambassade de ton pays avant de faire ta demande.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment demander l'asile au Canada?", "demande d'asile canada ou la faire", "refugie au canada comment faire"], a: "Tu peux demander l'asile à un point d'entrée (aéroport, frontière terrestre, port) à ton arrivée, ou de l'intérieur du Canada en ligne auprès d'IRCC. On vérifie d'abord si ta demande est recevable; si oui, elle est déférée à la Commission de l'immigration et du statut de réfugié (CISR), qui tient une audience. Obtiens de l'aide juridique rapidement : plusieurs provinces offrent l'aide juridique aux demandeurs d'asile.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi la CISR?", "commission de l'immigration et du statut de refugie", "qui decide des demandes d'asile"], a: "La Commission de l'immigration et du statut de réfugié du Canada (CISR) est un tribunal indépendant. Sa Section de la protection des réfugiés entend les demandes d'asile et décide si une personne est un réfugié au sens de la Convention ou une personne à protéger. D'autres sections s'occupent des appels et du contrôle des motifs de détention, y compris la Section d'appel de l'immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Je peux demander l'asile en arrivant des États-Unis par la route?", "entente sur les tiers pays surs", "traverser la frontiere pour demander l'asile"], a: "En vertu de l'Entente sur les tiers pays sûrs, les personnes qui arrivent des États-Unis par la frontière terrestre ne sont généralement pas admissibles à demander l'asile au Canada. Il y a des exceptions, par exemple certains membres de la famille au Canada ou les mineurs non accompagnés. Comme ça s'applique à toute la frontière terrestre, obtiens des conseils juridiques avant de voyager.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Qui a le droit de me faire payer pour des conseils en immigration?", "representant autorise immigration", "c'est quoi un CRIC"], a: "Seuls les représentants autorisés peuvent te faire payer :\n- Les consultants réglementés en immigration canadienne (CRIC), titulaires d'un permis du Collège des consultants en immigration et en citoyenneté\n- Les avocats membres d'un barreau provincial ou territorial\n- Les notaires membres de la Chambre des notaires du Québec\nUn ami ou un membre de ta famille peut t'aider gratuitement. Vérifie toujours le permis dans le registre officiel de l'organisme.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi un consultant fantôme?", "consultant non autorise immigration", "mon agent veut pas mettre son nom sur ma demande"], a: "Un consultant fantôme, c'est quelqu'un qui se fait payer pour t'aider sans être autorisé, et qui cache souvent son rôle en ne s'inscrivant pas comme représentant. Signes d'alerte :\n- Il refuse de signer comme représentant\n- Il garantit un visa ou un emploi\n- Il te demande de mentir ou de signer des formulaires en blanc\nC'est toi qui es responsable de ta demande. Fais affaire avec un CRIC ou un avocat, ou fais ta demande toi-même.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Est-ce que j'ai besoin d'un consultant pour faire ma demande?", "je peux faire ma demande de RP moi-meme?", "obligé de prendre un avocat?"], a: "Non. Tu peux faire ta demande toi-même : les formulaires et guides d'IRCC sont conçus pour ça, et passer par un représentant ne donne aucun traitement de faveur. Un consultant réglementé en immigration canadienne (CRIC) ou un avocat en immigration peut être utile dans les cas complexes : refus antérieurs, antécédents criminels, problèmes médicaux ou situation familiale compliquée.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Quelqu'un d'IRCC m'appelle et menace de m'arrêter, c'est vrai?", "appel frauduleux immigration", "on me demande de payer en cartes-cadeaux"], a: "C'est presque certainement une arnaque. IRCC ne t'appellera jamais pour te menacer d'arrestation ou d'expulsion, exiger un paiement immédiat ou te demander de payer en cartes-cadeaux, en cryptomonnaie ou par virement. Raccroche, ne donne aucun renseignement personnel et vérifie en passant par les canaux officiels. Signale l'arnaque au Centre antifraude du Canada et à ta police locale.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["C'est quoi les services d'établissement?", "aide gratuite pour les nouveaux arrivants", "organismes pour immigrants pres de chez moi"], a: "Les services d'établissement sont des services gratuits pour les nouveaux arrivants. Hors Québec, ils sont financés par IRCC; au Québec, c'est le gouvernement du Québec qui les finance et les organise. Ils peuvent t'aider à :\n- Chercher un emploi et faire reconnaître tes diplômes\n- Suivre des cours de français ou d'anglais gratuits\n- Trouver un logement et comprendre les services\n- Créer des liens dans ta communauté\nCertains services sont même offerts avant ton arrivée.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Je viens d'arriver au Canada, je fais quoi en premier?", "premieres etapes nouvel arrivant", "quoi faire apres l'atterrissage"], a: "Bienvenue au Canada! Les premières étapes habituelles :\n1. Obtenir ton numéro d'assurance sociale (NAS) auprès de Service Canada.\n2. Demander ta carte d'assurance maladie provinciale (au Québec, auprès de la RAMQ).\n3. Ouvrir un compte bancaire.\n4. Avoir un numéro de cellulaire canadien.\n5. Commencer à bâtir ton historique de crédit.\n6. Contacter un organisme d'aide aux nouveaux arrivants.\nGarde tes documents d'immigration en lieu sûr.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment obtenir mon NAS?", "numero d'assurance sociale nouvel arrivant", "ou demander le NAS"], a: "Tu demandes ton numéro d'assurance sociale (NAS) à Service Canada, en ligne, en personne ou par la poste. Apporte ton document principal : confirmation de résidence permanente, carte RP, ou permis de travail ou d'études valide. Le NAS est nécessaire pour travailler et pour certaines prestations. Garde-le confidentiel et donne-le seulement quand la loi l'exige, comme à ton employeur.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment avoir ma carte d'assurance maladie?", "RAMQ nouvel arrivant", "carte soleil immigrant delai de carence"], a: "Les soins de santé sont gérés par chaque province ou territoire, donc tu t'inscris auprès de ton régime provincial — au Québec, c'est la RAMQ. Tu dois généralement fournir tes documents d'immigration et une preuve d'adresse. Certaines provinces, dont le Québec, peuvent avoir un délai de carence avant le début de la couverture, alors prends une assurance privée en attendant. Vérifie les règles actuelles auprès de ta province.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Comment bâtir mon crédit au Canada?", "pas d'historique de credit nouvel arrivant", "cote de credit immigrant"], a: "Ton historique de crédit étranger ne suit généralement pas, alors tu repars à zéro. Quelques astuces :\n- Obtiens une carte de crédit (garantie ou pour nouveaux arrivants) et paie le solde au complet chaque mois.\n- Garde ton solde bas par rapport à ta limite.\n- Paie tes factures, comme ton cellulaire, à temps.\nTu peux consulter ton dossier de crédit gratuitement auprès des agences d'évaluation du crédit.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Y a-t-il des cours de français gratuits pour les immigrants?", "francisation quebec", "cours de francais gratuits nouvel arrivant"], a: "Oui! Au Québec, Francisation Québec offre des cours de français gratuits aux personnes admissibles, à temps plein ou à temps partiel, en personne ou en ligne, et certaines personnes peuvent recevoir une aide financière. Ailleurs au Canada, IRCC finance des cours de langue gratuits pour les nouveaux arrivants admissibles, en français (CLIC) ou en anglais (LINC). Contacte un organisme local pour t'inscrire.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Ma demande de visa a été refusée, je fais quoi?", "refus visa visiteur canada", "visa refusé pourquoi"], a: "D'abord, comprends pourquoi : lis la lettre de refus et pense à demander tes notes du SMGC pour voir le raisonnement de l'agent. Ensuite, tu peux :\n- Présenter une nouvelle demande plus solide qui répond aux préoccupations\n- Dans certains cas, demander un contrôle judiciaire à la Cour fédérale (délais stricts)\nUn refus ne t'interdit pas de refaire une demande, mais tu dois le déclarer. Pour des refus répétés, consulte un CRIC ou un avocat en immigration.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
  { q: ["Je viens d'avoir la confirmation de résidence permanente, c'est quoi la suite?", "c'est quoi une CRP", "confirmation de residence permanente expire"], a: "La confirmation de résidence permanente (CRP) est le document qu'IRCC te remet quand ta demande est approuvée. Tu deviens résident permanent une fois les formalités d'établissement terminées, soit à un point d'entrée, soit en ligne si tu es déjà au Canada. Ta CRP a une date d'expiration, alors assure-toi de t'établir avant. Ta carte RP te sera ensuite envoyée par la poste.", lang: "fr", topic: "immigration" },
];
